    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm studying for my ground school exam and im struggling a bit with lift. can u explain how bernoulli's principle and newton's third law work together to actually make a wing generate lift? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Bernoulli's principle explains that the curved shape of a wing causes air traveling over the top to move faster than the air underneath, resulting in lower pressure above. Newton's Third Law comes into play as the wing deflects some of this air downwards (downwash). Essentially, the faster air creates a pressure difference, while the downward deflection of the air exerts an equal and opposite upward force, which is lift. Together, these principles create the necessary upward force to keep the aircraft airborne.",
      "source": "target",
